# Who gets the review request.
#
# **One line, and the single line is the point rather than a placeholder.** There is one maintainer
# - `GOVERNANCE.md` says so and says what follows from it - so every path here resolves to the same
# person and this file distributes nothing. What it does instead is make that fact structural: a
# reader who opens CODEOWNERS expecting to find out who owns the token code finds out, in the same
# breath, that the answer is the same for every directory in the tree. That is a bus factor of 1
# stated where somebody evaluating the project will actually look, rather than only in a document
# they have to already have found.
#
# It also does one real job today: GitHub requests the review automatically, so a pull request from
# outside is not waiting on somebody noticing it exists.
#
# **Add per-path owners the day there is a second maintainer, not before.** A CODEOWNERS full of
# paths that all name one account is the shape this repository keeps refusing - configuration that
# describes a behaviour the project does not have. The paths worth splitting first, when there is
# somebody to split them with: `src/Boltway.OAuth.Tokens/` and `src/Boltway.OAuth.Net/`, because
# that is where a mistake mints or accepts a credential.

